{"answerText":"**No.** This NYC Building Code section does not prove that New York State Homes and Community Renewal (HCR) requires a vanity. It addresses water-closet clearance where only a forward approach is provided: a 66-inch minimum rear-wall clearance and 48-inch minimum side-wall clearance. It says a compliant **lavatory** is *permitted* on the rear wall at least 18 inches from the water-closet centerline; permission to locate a lavatory is not a requirement to provide one, and the text does not mention a **vanity**. (BC § 1107.2.2.7.2.2)

The provision is within the Type B+NYC toilet-and-bathing-room rules, so it applies only if that scope applies. Although its ancestor provision requires at least one compliant lavatory in each applicable Type B+NYC toilet or bathing room, that is an NYC Building Code requirement—not evidence of an HCR vanity requirement. (BC § 1107.2.2)

To determine whether HCR, a funding program, or agency guidance independently requires a vanity, the applicable HCR standard, funding requirements, or official guidance must be provided.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Forward-approach rule permits a lavatory location","explanation":"Where only a forward approach is provided to the water closet, the section requires the stated 66-inch by 48-inch minimum clearance. It permits—not requires—a lavatory complying with BC Section 1107.2.2.5 on the rear wall, at least 18 inches from the water-closet centerline. The selected text does not mention or regulate a vanity.","sectionID":"2713","sourceIDs":["befa5148-c553-48b4-9f01-5134e2a35112"]},{"heading":"Type B+NYC rooms separately address lavatories","explanation":"For toilet and bathing rooms provided in a Type B+NYC dwelling or sleeping unit, the ancestor provision requires at least one compliant lavatory and water closet in each applicable toilet room; bathing rooms also require either a bathtub or shower. This establishes an NYC Building Code lavatory rule only, not an HCR requirement for a vanity.","sectionID":"2702","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:2702-3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["Whether the toilet or bathing room is in a Type B+NYC dwelling unit or sleeping unit, which determines whether BC Section 1107.2.2 applies.","The applicable HCR design standard, funding-program requirements, or official HCR guidance that could independently require a vanity."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The assembled enacted evidence is limited to the 2022 NYC Construction Codes. It contains no HCR standard, funding requirement, or HCR guidance and therefore cannot establish an HCR-specific vanity requirement."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["The applicable HCR program/design standard, funding agreement or term sheet, and any official HCR guidance governing this project."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"2713","sourceIDs":["befa5148-c553-48b4-9f01-5134e2a35112"],"relevance":"Selected 2022 NYC Building Code text establishes the forward-approach clearance and conditional permission for a lavatory on the rear wall."},{"sectionID":"2702","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_cross_reference-id:2702-3"],"relevance":"Governing ancestor identifies the limited Type B+NYC scope and separately requires a compliant lavatory in applicable rooms."}]}

{"answerText":"**M1 is more permissive than M2 and M3 for the listed Use Group I open uses; M2 and M3 have the same allowances in this table.** Agricultural uses are permitted with additional conditions and open-use allowances in all three districts (●P U). Outdoor skating rinks are permitted in all three (●). Sand, gravel, or clay pits require a special permit in all three (○). Cemeteries, golf courses, outdoor racket courts, and public or private parks/playgrounds are permitted only in M1; they are not permitted in M2 or M3. (ZR § 42-111.)

The table symbols mean: **●** permitted; **♦** permitted with limitations; **○** special permit required; **–** not permitted; **S** size restriction; **P** additional conditions; and **U** open use allowances. Although ♦ and S do not appear in the listed M1/M2/M3 entries, they are part of the table’s legend. (ZR § 42-111.)

For uses listed as “Open Uses,” the table also states that where a use is permitted as-of-right in a Manufacturing District, it must be unenclosed, except for ancillary buildings or other structures. (ZR § 42-111.)","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"M1 compared with M2 and M3","explanation":"For the listed Use Group I entries, M2 and M3 match each other. Agricultural uses are shown as ●P U in M1, M2, and M3; outdoor skating rinks as ● in all three; and sand, gravel, or clay pits as ○ in all three. Cemeteries, golf courses, outdoor racket courts, and public or private parks/playgrounds are ● or ●P in M1 but – in M2 and M3. The table separately states that qualifying permitted-as-of-right uses listed as “Open Uses” must be unenclosed, except for ancillary buildings or other structures.","sectionID":"20017276","sourceIDs":["f6697dac-e906-4992-8431-10e565fb7375"]},{"heading":"Table-symbol meanings","explanation":"The legend defines ● as Permitted; ♦ as Permitted with limitations; ○ as Special permit required; – as Not permitted; S as Size restriction; P as Additional conditions; and U as Open use allowances.","sectionID":"20017276","sourceIDs":["f6697dac-e906-4992-8431-10e565fb7375"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":[],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["This summary is limited to the selected ZR § 42-111 table and its stated legend; it does not supply the separate provisions that may contain the referenced limitations, size restrictions, additional conditions, open-use allowances, or special-permit criteria."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20017276","sourceIDs":["f6697dac-e906-4992-8431-10e565fb7375"],"relevance":"Selected enacted Use Group I table, including the M1/M2/M3 entries, symbol legend, and unenclosed-open-use statement."}]}

