{"answerText":"The supplied current Zoning Resolution text does **not** contain NYC Planning amendment-history metadata for § 42-00, so that metadata cannot be identified from this enacted evidence. The current text identifies § 42-00 as the Manufacturing Districts’ general use-provisions framework: it classifies uses into Use Groups I–X, directs general use allowances to §§ 42-11 through 42-20, and states that Chapter 2 use provisions may be modified or superseded by specified special rules and Special Purpose Districts. (ZR § 42-00)

For text in force on a particular past date, a professional must verify the actual amendment instruments and their effective dates, then compare the version of § 42-00 in force immediately before and after each applicable amendment. They must also verify any applicable transition or permit provisions and the project facts that trigger them. The supplied text confirms that § 11-33 applies to qualifying development or construction authorized by building permits lawfully issued before an applicable amendment’s effective date, except where the Resolution specifically provides otherwise; it does not supply an amendment date, the historical § 42-00 text, or the asserted Planning metadata. (ZR § 11-33)

Accordingly, current metadata—even if obtained separately—would not by itself establish the historical operative text or a project’s transition status.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Current scope of ZR § 42-00","explanation":"The supplied continuously amended 2026 Zoning Resolution text describes § 42-00 as the general framework for Manufacturing District use regulation: Use Groups I through X; general allowances in §§ 42-11 through 42-20; specified special provisions; performance standards; supplementary use regulations; sign regulations; and potential modification or supersession by special rules and Special Purpose Districts.","sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["7756d03f-3a8f-4f47-b515-62a1631fbcd0"]},{"heading":"Permit transition provision has a fact-specific scope","explanation":"ZR § 11-33 states that its provisions apply to minor developments, major developments, or other construction authorized by building permits lawfully issued before the effective date of an applicable Zoning Resolution amendment, except as specifically provided elsewhere in the Resolution. The supplied passage does not provide the date or substantive terms of an amendment affecting § 42-00.","sectionID":"20018441","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:20018441-4"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The official NYC Planning amendment-history metadata for ZR § 42-00.","The particular historical date to be analyzed.","The enacted amendment instruments, effective dates, and successive historical text versions affecting ZR § 42-00 before that date.","If transition treatment is at issue: the applicable project or permit record, including whether a building permit was lawfully issued before the applicable amendment’s effective date, and any specifically applicable exception elsewhere in the Zoning Resolution."],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied enacted evidence is the continuously amended NYC Zoning Resolution text through 2026-08-13. It does not include NYC Planning amendment-history metadata, amendment instruments or effective dates, or historical versions of ZR § 42-00; therefore it cannot establish text in force on a particular historical date."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["Official NYC Planning amendment-history record for ZR § 42-00.","The relevant enacted amendment texts and their effective-date provisions.","For a project-specific transition determination, the relevant lawfully issued building-permit record and any applicable transition provisions or exceptions."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20017271","sourceIDs":["7756d03f-3a8f-4f47-b515-62a1631fbcd0"],"relevance":"Current enacted scope and stated potential modification or supersession of Chapter 2 use provisions."},{"sectionID":"20018441","sourceIDs":["research-permitext_discovered-id:20018441-4"],"relevance":"Supplied enacted text on the stated scope of the building-permit transition provision."}]}

{"answerText":"**No maximum permitted residential FAR can be selected** until the site’s mapped zoning district is established. The proposed building’s ratio is **4.0 FAR** (40,000 sq ft ÷ 10,000 sq ft), but that calculation does not establish that 4.0 FAR is permitted.

The Zoning Resolution makes the zoning maps—including amendments—part of the Resolution and uses them to establish district locations and boundaries (ZR § 11-14). Therefore, Bronx location alone does not identify the governing district or its residential FAR.

Provide the site address or BBL and the controlling official zoning-map or verified mapped-district evidence. The applicable district’s FAR rule would then be needed to compare the proposed 4.0 FAR with the permitted FAR.","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Zoning-map status controls district identification","explanation":"ZR § 11-14 provides that the locations and boundaries of districts established by the Resolution are shown on zoning maps, and that the maps in their entirety, including amendments, are incorporated into the Resolution. Because no mapped district for this site is established, the supplied enacted evidence does not support selecting a parcel-specific maximum residential FAR.","sectionID":"20018425","sourceIDs":["3006237a-f89d-4a23-b6d7-4d53dbd3956b"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":["The site address or BBL and the controlling official zoning-map or verified mapped-district status. This can determine the applicable residential FAR and whether the proposed 4.0 FAR is permissible.","The enacted FAR provision applicable to the verified mapped district, including any established special-district or other mapped-area status that changes the district’s FAR rule."],"followUpQuestions":["What is the site address or BBL, and what controlling official zoning-map evidence establishes its mapped zoning district?"],"evidenceLimitations":["The supplied enacted evidence establishes that zoning maps determine district locations and boundaries, but it does not identify this site’s mapped district or supply the residential FAR rule for that district."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":["Controlling official zoning-map or verified mapped-district evidence for the site.","The enacted residential FAR provision for the verified mapped district and any applicable mapped special-district area."],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20018425","sourceIDs":["3006237a-f89d-4a23-b6d7-4d53dbd3956b"],"relevance":"ZR § 11-14 establishes that zoning-map district locations and boundaries, including map amendments, are incorporated into the Zoning Resolution."}]}

{"answerText":"**0 accessory off-street residential parking spaces are required** for the 80 units, assuming the supplied Inner Transit Zone status. ZR § 25-211 provides that no accessory off-street parking spaces are required for dwelling units created after December 5, 2024 within the Inner Transit Zone. A unit is “created” when its temporary certificate of occupancy is issued, or, if none is issued, when its final certificate of occupancy is issued. Because every stated unit meets that post-December 5, 2024 certificate date, the calculation is **80 units × 0 spaces per unit = 0 spaces**. (ZR § 25-211)

ZR § 25-20 directs Inner Transit Zone residential parking to § 25-21. The different existing-unit route applies only to multiple-dwelling units created from July 20, 1950 through December 5, 2024, which must continue under § 25-212 unless reduced or eliminated under the cited provisions. It does not apply to these stipulated post-December 5, 2024 units. (ZR §§ 25-20, 25-211)

The stated absence of a qualifying pre-December 5, 2024 continuation under ZR § 11-333 means that transition route does not alter this result. (ZR § 11-333)","supportedPoints":[{"heading":"Post-December 5, 2024 Inner Transit Zone units require zero spaces","explanation":"Within the Inner Transit Zone, ZR § 25-211 requires no accessory off-street parking spaces for dwelling units created after December 5, 2024. Under the stipulated certificate dates, all 80 units meet that condition; 80 dwelling units × 0 required spaces per unit = 0 required spaces.","sectionID":"20017541","sourceIDs":["02295564-6dd6-4318-9ea7-e0cfb24b1e36"]},{"heading":"Certificate issuance determines when each unit is created","explanation":"For § 25-211, “created” means issuance of a temporary certificate of occupancy for the applicable unit, or issuance of a final certificate of occupancy where no temporary certificate has been issued. The supplied post-December 5, 2024 certificate premise therefore places the units in the zero-space rule.","sectionID":"20017541","sourceIDs":["02295564-6dd6-4318-9ea7-e0cfb24b1e36"]},{"heading":"Existing-unit parking route does not govern these units","explanation":"ZR § 25-20 directs Inner Transit Zone residential parking to § 25-21. Section 25-211 instead retains parking under § 25-212 for multiple-dwelling units created between July 20, 1950 and December 5, 2024, subject only to the specified reduction or removal provisions. That date range does not include the stipulated units.","sectionID":"20017540","sourceIDs":["11a6661b-6bb3-4f9e-aa71-88449fafd0b4"]},{"heading":"No stated Section 11-333 continuation applies","explanation":"ZR § 11-333 contains a continuation route for applications filed on or before December 5, 2024 and approved by December 5, 2025 under the pre-amendment rules. The hypothetical expressly states that no application qualifies for that continuation, so it does not alter the post-December 5, 2024 result under § 25-211.","sectionID":"20018444","sourceIDs":["0bc2b523-a9de-4ded-97b0-90ee14082ea3"]}],"assumptions":[],"missingFacts":[],"followUpQuestions":[],"evidenceLimitations":["This answer is limited to the supplied enacted NYC Zoning Resolution passages and the stipulated Inner Transit Zone and certificate-of-occupancy facts."],"additionalEvidenceNeeded":[],"supportingSourceUses":[],"citations":[{"sectionID":"20017541","sourceIDs":["02295564-6dd6-4318-9ea7-e0cfb24b1e36"],"relevance":"Establishes the Inner Transit Zone zero-parking rule after December 5, 2024, the definition of “created,” and the separate pre-December 5, 2024 existing-unit parking route."},{"sectionID":"20017540","sourceIDs":["11a6661b-6bb3-4f9e-aa71-88449fafd0b4"],"relevance":"Directs Inner Transit Zone residential parking requirements to ZR § 25-21."},{"sectionID":"20018444","sourceIDs":["0bc2b523-a9de-4ded-97b0-90ee14082ea3"],"relevance":"Provides the stated pre-December 5, 2024 continuation path that the hypothetical says does not apply."}]}

{"answerText":"**0 accessory off-street residential parking spaces are required** for the 80 units, assuming the supplied Inner Transit Zone status. ZR § 25-211 provides that no accessory off-street parking spaces are required for dwelling units created after December 5, 2024 within the Inner Transit Zone. A unit is “created” when its temporary certificate of occupancy is issued, or, if no temporary certificate of occupancy is issued, when its final certificate of occupancy is issued. Because every stated unit’s applicable temporary certificate of occupancy—or final certificate where no temporary certificate is issued—will be issued after December 5, 2024, all 80 units are created after December 5, 2024. The calculation is **80 units × 0 spaces per unit = 0 spaces**. (ZR § 25-211)

ZR § 25-20 directs Inner Transit Zone residential parking to § 25-21. The different existing-unit route applies only to multiple-dwelling units created between July 20, 1950 and December 5, 2024: parking for those units must continue to be provided under § 25-212, and the number required under § 25-212 may only be reduced or eliminated under the specified provisions. That existing-unit parking route does not apply to these units created after December 5, 2024. (ZR §§ 25-20, 25-211, 25-212)

Section 11-333’s stated continuation route concerns an application filed on or before **December 5, 2024** and approved by the Department of Buildings on or before **December 5, 2025** under rules existing before the December 5, 2024 adoption. Because the question states that no application qualifies for that pre-December 5, 2024 continuation, it does not alter the § 25-211 result for these post-December 5, 2024 units. (ZR § 11-333)","supportedPointUpserts":[{"targetIndex":2,"value":{"heading":"Existing-unit parking route does not govern these units","explanation":"ZR § 25-20 directs Inner Transit Zone residential parking to § 25-21. Section 25-211 retains parking under § 25-212 for multiple-dwelling units created between July 20, 1950 and December 5, 2024; the number required pursuant to § 25-212 may only be reduced or eliminated under the specified provisions. That date range does not include the stipulated units, which are created after December 5, 2024.","sectionID":"20017541","sourceIDs":["11a6661b-6bb3-4f9e-aa71-88449fafd0b4","02295564-6dd6-4318-9ea7-e0cfb24b1e36"]}}],"supportedPointRemovals":[],"citationUpserts":[{"targetIndex":2,"value":{"sectionID":"20018444","sourceIDs":["0bc2b523-a9de-4ded-97b0-90ee14082ea3"],"relevance":"Section 11-333 permits continuation where an application was filed on or before December 5, 2024 and approved on or before December 5, 2025 under the pre-December 5, 2024 rules; the question stipulates that no application qualifies."}}],"citationRemovals":[],"missingFactsAdd":[],"missingFactsRemove":[],"evidenceLimitationsAdd":[],"evidenceLimitationsRemove":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededAdd":[],"additionalEvidenceNeededRemove":[]}
